{"level":30,"time":1776272584442,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":4,"prompt":"Please examine Sheet 4 (Existing Conditions Survey) and identify:\n1. Are there any existing utility poles (UTILITY POLE symbol) shown on the property or adjacent to it?\n2. Are there any overhead utility lines (O.H.U.L. - OVERHEAD UTILITY LINE symbol) shown on the property or adjacent to it?\n3. Are there any electric easements (ELECTRIC EASEMENT symbol) shown?\n4. What is the location relative to the site boundaries (north, south, east, west, adjacent)?\n5. If any poles or overhead lines exist, describe their routing and proximity to proposed buildings or site features.\n\nThe legend shows these symbols are used on this survey, so carefully examine the entire drawing for their presence.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of Sheet 4, the \"Existing Conditions Survey,\" here are the findings regarding your specific questions:\n\n**1. Are there any existing utility poles shown on the property or adjacent to it?**\nYes. There are at least three existing utility poles shown within the property boundaries. They are denoted by the circle-with-a-cross symbol defined in the legend as \"UTILITY POLE\".\n\n**2. Are there any overhead utility lines shown on the property or adjacent to it?**\nYes. Overhead utility lines are shown on the property. They are indicated by a line type containing the text \"- OH -\", which corresponds to the \"- OH - OVERHEAD UTILITY LINE\" entry in the legend.\n\n**3. Are there any electric easements shown?**\nYes. Electric easements are explicitly labeled on the drawing with dashed lines indicating their boundaries.\n*   One is labeled **\"5' ELEC. EASEMENT VOL. 8011, PG. 74 P.R.T.C.TX\"**\n*   Another is labeled **\"5' ELEC. EASEMENT VOL. 548, PG. 885 D.R.T.C.TX\"**\n\n**4. What is the location relative to the site boundaries (north, south, east, west, adjacent)?**\n*   **Utility Poles & Overhead Lines:** These are located just inside the **northern** property boundary (the boundary bearing \"S63°02'28\"E 419.86'\"). They run roughly parallel to this line.\n*   **Electric Easements:**\n    *   The \"5' ELEC. EASEMENT VOL. 8011, PG. 74 P.R.T.C.TX\" runs parallel to the **northern** property boundary.\n    *   The \"5' ELEC. EASEMENT VOL. 548, PG. 885 D.R.T.C.TX\" runs parallel to the **eastern** property boundary (the boundary bearing \"S27°19'28\"W 146.15'\").\n\n**5. If any poles or overhead lines exist, describe their routing and proximity to proposed buildings or site features.**\n*Note: As this is an \"Existing Conditions Survey,\" it displays existing site features rather than proposed buildings. Proximity is described relative to the existing features shown.*\n\n*   **Routing:** The overhead utility lines (\"- OH -\") enter the property from the west (Valley View Road) near the northwest corner. They route eastwards across the northern portion of the site, staying relatively close to the northern property line, and exit the property near the northeast corner. This routing is supported by at least three utility poles spaced along the line.\n*   **Proximity:**\n    *   **Trees:** The lines pass directly through the canopies of several existing trees along the northern edge of the property (e.g., passing over or immediately adjacent to trees marked 4022, 4026, 4038, and a cluster near the northeast corner).\n    *   **Existing Structures:** The lines run north of the main existing building footprint (labeled **\"SLAB F.F.E.=686.8'\"**). They pass in close proximity to the northernmost edge of the associated concrete flatwork (labeled **\"CONC.\"**) and pass very near a **\"CONC. COLUMN\"** located on the northwest side of the existing slab area.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTok