UHLENBERGII LINDHEIMERII | 1 GALLON | SEE PLANS |\n   227→| [GC] | 29 | GULF MUHLY (1 GALLON), MUHLENBERGIA CAPILLARIS 'REGAL MIST' | 1 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n   228→| [LS] | 8 | TEXAS SAGE, LEUCOPHYLLUM FRUTESCENS 'GREEN CLOUD' | 7 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n   229→| [MC] | 8 | DWARF WAX MYRTLE, MYRICA CERIFERA 'DON'S DWARF' | 7 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n   230→\n   231→## Block 20: text_block — Project Location details.\n   232→\n   233→PROJECT LOCATION:\n   234→3759 VALLEY\n   235→VIEW ROAD,\n   236→AUSTIN,\n   237→TEXAS 78704\n   238→\n   239→## Block 21: text_block — Owner details.\n   240→\n   241→OWNER:\n   242→WM 41 AUSTIN\n   243→RE, LLC\n   244→\n   245→## Block 23: text_block — Contact information for Blu Fish Collaborative Architecture.\n   246→\n   247→107 Leland Street, Suite 2\n   248→Austin, TX 78704\n   249→P.O. BOX 40792\n   250→Austin, TX 78704\n   251→Phone: (512) 388-4115\n   252→\n   253→## Block 24: text_block — Project Name details.\n   254→\n   255→PROJECT NAME:\n   256→VALLEY VIEW\n   257→TOWNHOMES\n   258→\n   259→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d1a7ac5-a447-4e3a-aebe-824ab80a6536","uuid":"d901937b-14e0-4242-92f2-3bede69792d6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-35/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 35 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: notes — Notes related to tree preservation calculations.\n\nNOTES:\n1. CHINABERRY AND BRADFORD PEAR ARE INVASIVE TREES AND REQUIRE NO MITIGATION.\n2. ASH TREES REQUIRE NO MITIGATION DUE TO EMERALD ASH BORER.\n\n## Block 3: notes — Notes on special construction techniques.\n\nSPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\nECM 3.5.4 (D)\n1. PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF\nTREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A\nCLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES\nWITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n2. IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING\nCONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC\nIS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF\nORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH\nSOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION\n620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE\nMIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS\nPLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS, COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY\nARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n3. ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE\nREMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3\nINCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY\nHAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n4. WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION\nACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE\nLEAVES.\n5. WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE,\nUSE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT\nLEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n\n## Block 5: notes — Plant list section heading.\n\nPLANT LIST\n\n## Block 6: notes — Notes related to the plant list.\n\nNOTES:\n1. THE CONTRACTORS (GENERAL AND SUBCONTRACTORS) SHALL PROVIDE UNIT COSTS FOR ALL\nSOFTSCAPE AND HARDSCAPE MATERIAL SPECIFIED ON THE DRAWINGS AND SPECIFICATIONS. UNIT\nCOSTS SHALL BE PROVIDED FOR MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION SEPARATELY. UNIT COSTS SHALL\nBE: 'EACH' FOR PLANT MATERIAL, 'SQUARE FOOT' OR 'SQUARE YARD' FOR PAVEMENTS, LINEAR FOOT\nFOR WALL AND FENCE, 'EACH' FOR SITE FURNISHINGS AND SITE AMENITIES, 'CUBIC YARD' OR 'CUBIC\nFOOT' FOR SOIL, MULCH AND OTHER BULK PRODUCTS AND 'EACH' OR 'LUMP SUM' FOR\nMISCELLANEOUS ITEMS. THE UNIT COST SHALL BE FORMATTED TO HAVE COLUMNS FOR; ITEMS, UNIT,\nUNIT COST, TOTAL ITEM COST.\n2. PLANT QUANTITIES ARE PROVIDED FOR OWNER CONVENIENCE ONLY. CONTRACTOR IS\nRESPONSIBLE FOR VERIFYING AND PROVIDING PLANT QUANTITIES SHOWN ON THE LANDSCAPE PLAN.\n3. CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR LOCATING AND SECURING TREE AND PLANT MATERIAL IN\nADVANCE. BECAUSE OF THE LONG LEAD TIME ON THIS INSTALLATION AND THE RECENT SHORTAGE\nON TREE CROP, CONTRACTOR SHALL RESERVE OR CONTRACT GROW TREES WITH A GROWER.\n\n## Block 11: table — Header for City of Austin Tree Preservation Calculations.\n\nCITY OF AUSTIN\nTREE PRESERVATION CALCULATIONS:\n\n## Block 12: table — Calculations related to tree preservation.\n\nEXISTING TREES:\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 212 INCHES\n(HERITAGE)\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 0 INCHES\n(NON HERITAGE)\n19 TO 23.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES 99 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES 295 INCHES\n19 INCHES AND GREATER, ALL OTHER TREES 0 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ALL OTHER TREES 66 INCHES\n\nTREES REMOVED\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 27 INCHES\n(HERITAGE):\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 0 INCHES\n(NON HERITAGE):\n19 TO 23.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES: 79 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES: 134 INCHES\n19 INCHES AND GREATER, ALL OTHER TREES: 0 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ALL OTHER TREES: 66 INCHES\nTOTAL: 288 INCHES\n\nREPLACEMENT INCHES REQUIRED\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 40.5 INCHES\n(HERITAGE): 300%\n24 INCHES AND GREATER, ECM APP. F TREES 0 INCHES\n(NON HERITAGE): 100%\n19 TO 23.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES: 100% 79 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ECM APP. F TREES: 50% 82 INCHES\n19 INCHES AND GREATER, ALL OTHER TREES: 50% 0 INCHES\n8 TO 18.9 INCHES, ALL OTHER TREES: 25% 15.5 INCHES\nTOTAL: 217 INCHES\n\nURBAN FORESTRY INFORMATION\nTOTAL APP F TREE INCHES SURVEYED 606 INCHES\nTOTAL APP F TREE INCHES REMOVED 240 INCHES\nTOTAL INVASIVE TREE INCHES REMOVED 137 INCHES\n\nTOTAL MITIGATION INCHES PLANTED ON SITE 55 INCHES\nREMAINING MITIGATION INCHES REQUIRED 162 INCHES\n\nMITIGATION DEFICIT (REPLACE REMAINING AT $32,400\nSTANDARD $200 PER INCH)\nHB7 MITIGATION CREDIT (55x50%x$200) $5,500\nAMOUNT TO BE PAID INTO FUND $26,900\n\n## Block 13: table — Table listing shade trees.\n\nSHADE TREES\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n\n## Block 14: table — Chart detailing required trees.\n\nCOA REQUIRED TREES CHART\n| | NUMBER OF TREES | INCHES OF TREES |\n|---|---|---|\n| STREETYARD | 5 TREES @ 1.5\" | 7.5 |\n| BUFFER | 0 LARGE TREES @ 4\" | 0 |\n| | 1 SMALL TREES @ 2.5\" | 2.5 |\n| PARKING LOT | 2 TREES @ 1.5\" | 3 |\n| | Total tree inches required | A = 13 |\n\nPROPOSED\n| PER PLANT LIST | 12 @ 4\" | B = 68 |\n| | 8 @ 2.5\" | |\n\nPROPOSED TREE INCHES THAT MAY BE COUNTED FOR MITIGATION\n| | B-A = C | C = 55 |\n\n* Every required tree must be at least 1.5 caliper inches.\n\n## Block 15: table — Calculations related to landscaping.\n\nCITY OF AUSTIN\nLANDSCAPE CALCULATIONS:\n\nSTREET YARD AREA:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| TOTAL SITE AREA | N/A | 94,203 SF |\n| TOTAL STREET-YARD AREA | N/A | 6,314 SF |\n| STREET-YARD / LANDSCAPE (20%) | 1,263 SF | 5,206 SF |\n\nSTREET YARD TREES:\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2\" DIAMETER TO 6\" DIAMETER | 0 | |\n| 6\" DIAMETER OR GREATER | 4 | |\n\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 8 CREDITS |\n\nPROPOSED TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 0 CREDITS |\n\nTOTAL STREET YARD TREES, EXISTING TREES CREDIT PLUS PROPOSED TREES CREDIT:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| | 5 | 8 |\n\nLANDSCAPE IN PARKING LOTS:\n90SF / 12 PARKING SPACES LOCATED IN SY\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| | 0 | 0 |\n\n60SF / 12 PARKING SPACES LOCATED IN NON SY\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| | 60 | 816 |\n\nLANDSCAPE ISLAND MUST BE WITHIN 50' OF EACH SPACE, 8' MIN. INSIDE WIDTH\n1 TREE MUST BE WITHIN 50' OF EACH SPACE\n\nBUFFERING POINTS FOR DRIVE AISLE:\n| | SIZE | QTY | PREFERRED | OTHER | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LARGE TREE | 4\" | 0 | YES | | 0 |\n| SMALL TREE | 2.5\" | 1 | YES | | 9 |\n| MEDIUM SHRUB | 5 GAL. | 0 | YES | | 0 |\n| LARGE SHRUB | 7 GAL. | 16 | YES | | 48 |\n\nBUFFER POINTS REQUIRED: 40\nBUFFER POINTS PROVIDED: 57\n\nPRIVATE COMMON OPEN SPACE & PEDESTRIAN AMENITIES: SUB CHAPTER E\nREQUIRED (5% OF SITE AREA) (5% X 94,293) 4,715 SF\nPROVIDED 5,855 SF\n\n## Block 16: table — Table listing groundcovers.\n\nGROUNDCOVERS\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. CONTAINER | MAX. SPACING |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [dotted pattern] | 92 | OBEDIENT PLANT, PHYSTOSTEGIA VIRGINIANA | 4\" FLAT | 2' O.C. MAX. |\n| [grid pattern] | 72 | FROGFRUIT, PHYLA NODIFLORA | 4\" FLAT | 2' O.C. MAX. |\n\n## Block 17: table — Table listing ornamental trees.\n\nORNAMENTAL TREES\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [circle with wavy edge] | 2 | TEXAS MOUNTAIN LAUREL, SOPHORA SECUNDIFLORA | 45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 8' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n| [circle with jagged edge] | 6 | MEXICAN REDBUD, CERCIS CANADENSIS VAR. MEXICANA | 45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 10' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n\n## Block 18: table — Table listing shrubs.\n\nSHRUBS\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. CONTAINER | MAX. SPACING |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [PM] | 12 | DWARF PALMETTO, SABAL MINOR | 5 GALLON | 4' O.C. |\n| [MB] | 24 | BIG MUHLY (1 gal), MUHLENBERGII LINDHEIMERII | 1 GALLON | SEE PLANS |\n| [GC] | 29 | GULF MUHLY (1 GALLON), MUHLENBERGIA CAPILLARIS 'REGAL MIST' | 1 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n| [LS] | 8 | TEXAS SAGE, LEUCOPHYLLUM FRUTESCENS 'GREEN CLOUD' | 7 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n| [MC] | 8 | DWARF WAX MYRTLE, MYRICA CERIFERA 'DON'S DWARF' | 7 GALLON | 3' O.C. |\n\n## Block 20: text_block — Project Location details.\n\nPROJECT LOCATION:\n3759 VALLEY\nVIEW ROAD,\nAUSTIN,\nTEXAS 78704\n\n## Block 21: text_block — Owner details.\n\nOWNER:\nWM 41 AUSTIN\nRE, LLC\n\n## Block 23: text_block — Contact information for Blu Fish Collaborative Architecture.\n\n107 Leland Street, Suite 2\nAustin, TX 78704\nP.O. BOX 40792\nAustin, TX 78704\nPhone: (512) 388-4115\n\n## Block 24: text_block — Project Name details.\n\nPROJECT NAME:\nVALLEY VIEW\nTOWNHOMES\n\n","numLines":259,"startLine":1,"totalLines":259}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776272591420,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01K3ybCBDBedWBD5qzbpBxWD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014vp1Usqx24qNTjTFd6qchS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/guide.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1842,"cache_read_input_tokens":40828,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1842},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"210f7c66-b6ac-4c5e-b337-405e381dc733","uuid":"770ab79d-4b17-4ae1-bc9b-d252fc98835e"}
{"level":30,"time":1776272591520,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014vp1Usqx24qNTjTFd6qchS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Site Plan\n     2→\n     3→Detailed site plan layout with associated building and parking data.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the primary Site Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project (Sheet 9 of 39). It illustrates the overall site layout, including the arrangement of eight multi-family residential buildings, internal driveways, parking, a detention pond, pedestrian walkways, and retaining walls, while providing comprehensive tabular data to verify compliance with zoning, area, coverage, height, and parking requirements.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Site Area:** 94,192 sf (2.162 acres)\n    11→*   **Zoning:** MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density\n    12→*   **Total Units:** 39 units (16 Two Bedroom, 23 Three Bedroom) across 8 buildings\n    13→*   **Maximum Building Height:** 40' allowed; 36' - 1\" is the highest proposed (Building 3)\n    14→*   **Impervious Cover:** 59% proposed (55,502 sf) vs 60% allowable\n    15→*   **Building Coverage:** 34% proposed (32,296 sf) vs 50% allowable\n    16→*   **Gross Floor Area:** 83,986 sf\n    17→*   **Vehicle Parking:** 88 total spaces provided (10 surface, 78 garage)\n    18→*   **Bicycle Parking:** 5 required; 41 provided (39 long term, 2 short term)\n    19→*   **Accessibility Slopes:** Max parking slope 1:50; Max cross-slope 1:50; Max ramp slope 1:12\n    20→*   **Benchmarks (NAVD 88):** BM #1 = 678.52'; BM #2 = 681.08'\n    21→\n    22→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    23→\n    24→*   **Block 1: drawing — Main Site Plan**\n    25→    A comprehensive layout drawing of the property. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout of Buildings 1-8, the routing of internal driveways and concrete/decomposed granite walkways, the location of the biofiltration/detention pond in the northeast corner, and the placement of retaining walls around the site perimeter.\n    26→\n    27→*   **Block 16: table — Site Data Table**\n    28→    A summary table of overall site metrics. READ the block content for exact values verifying zoning compliance, including site area, existing vs. proposed impervious cover percentages, allowable vs. proposed building coverage, and gross floor area.\n    29→\n    30→*   **Block 14: table — Building Data Table**\n    31→    A breakdown of metrics per building. READ the block content for individual building types (4-, 5-, or 6-plexes), gross floor areas, building footprints, bedroom configurations, and total unit counts (39 total, maximum 3 stories).\n    32→\n    33→*   **Block 15: table — Building Height Table**\n    34→    A summary of building heights for compliance checking. READ the block content for the average MSL, physical building heights (ranging from 33'-4.75\" to 36'-1\"), and verification that all buildings fall under the 40' allowable height limit.\n    35→\n    36→*   **Block 17: table — Site Area Calculation (MF-2)**\n    37→    A calculation of required area based on unit types. READ the block content to verify that the 94,192 sf provided site area satisfies the 93,600 sf required for the 39 2+ bedroom units.\n    38→\n    39→*   **Block 8: notes — Parking Data**\n    40→    A tabular summary of parking requirements. READ the block content for the breakdown of vehicle parking (surface vs. garage) and the exact counts for short-term and long-term bicycle parking spaces.\n    41→\n    42→*   **Block 10: notes — Site Plan Release Notes**\n    43→    A list of administrative conditions for the site plan. READ the block content for compliance requirements regarding standard building codes, signage, utility providers, demolition, and right-of-way excavation permits.\n    44→\n    45→*   **Block 4: notes — Accessibility Notes**\n    46→    Design constraints for ADA compliance. READ the block content for specific, mandated slope tolerances for accessible parking spaces, cross-slopes, and ramps (e.g., max ramp slope of 1:12).\n    47→\n    48→*   **Block 2: drawing — Lighting Note & Figure 34**\n    49→    Requirements for exterior lighting. READ for the requirement that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded per Subchapter E 2.5. Use VISION to review the four acceptable fixture profiles illustrated in Figure 34.\n    50→\n    51→*   **Block 6: notes — Fence Note**\n    52→    Tree protection requirements. READ the block content for instructions mandating hand-digging of post holes within Critical Root Zones (CRZ) and the required application of 3-inch hardwood mulch.\n    53→\n    54→*   **Block 9: notes — Trash Collection Notes**\n    55→    Rules for waste management. READ the block content for curbside unattended container restrictions and necessary enforcement signage requirements.\n    56→\n    57→*   **Blocks 3, 5, 7, 11, 12, 13: various — Administrative & Boilerplate Data**\n    58→    Standard site plan identifiers and administrative data. READ for the legal description (Block 3), the specific benchmark elevations (Block 5), and general utility liability notes (Block 7). Use VISION to locate the graphic scale showing 1\" = 20' (Block 11), the Texas 811 call-before-you-dig logo (Block 12), and the engineer's seal dated 2/27/26 (Block 13).\n    59→\n    60→---\n    61→\n    62→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    63→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"210f7c66-b6ac-4c5e-b337-405e381dc733","uuid":"4bbf7b79-3de0-4a89-b26b-82cedec4f618","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Site Plan\n\nDetailed site plan layout with associated building and parking data.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the primary Site Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project (Sheet 9 of 39). It illustrates the overall site layout, including the arrangement of eight multi-family residential buildings, internal driveways, parking, a detention pond, pedestrian walkways, and retaining walls, while providing comprehensive tabular data to verify compliance with zoning, area, coverage, height, and parking requirements.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Site Area:** 94,192 sf (2.162 acres)\n*   **Zoning:** MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density\n*   **Total Units:** 39 units (16 Two Bedroom, 23 Three Bedroom) across 8 buildings\n*   **Maximum Building Height:** 40' allowed; 36' - 1\" is the highest proposed (Building 3)\n*   **Impervious Cover:** 59% proposed (55,502 sf) vs 60% allowable\n*   **Building Coverage:** 34% proposed (32,296 sf) vs 50% allowable\n*   **Gross Floor Area:** 83,986 sf\n*   **Vehicle Parking:** 88 total spaces provided (10 surface, 78 garage)\n*   **Bicycle Parking:** 5 required; 41 provided (39 long term, 2 short term)\n*   **Accessibility Slopes:** Max parking slope 1:50; Max cross-slope 1:50; Max ramp slope 1:12\n*   **Benchmarks (NAVD 88):** BM #1 = 678.52'; BM #2 = 681.08'\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 1: drawing — Main Site Plan**\n    A comprehensive layout drawing of the property. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout of Buildings 1-8, the routing of internal driveways and concrete/decomposed granite walkways, the location of the biofiltration/detention pond in the northeast corner, and the placement of retaining walls around the site perimeter.\n\n*   **Block 16: table — Site Data Table**\n    A summary table of overall site metrics. READ the block content for exact values verifying zoning compliance, including site area, existing vs. proposed impervious cover percentages, allowable vs. proposed building coverage, and gross floor area.\n\n*   **Block 14: table — Building Data Table**\n    A breakdown of metrics per building. READ the block content for individual building types (4-, 5-, or 6-plexes), gross floor areas, building footprints, bedroom configurations, and total unit counts (39 total, maximum 3 stories).\n\n*   **Block 15: table — Building Height Table**\n    A summary of building heights for compliance checking. READ the block content for the average MSL, physical building heights (ranging from 33'-4.75\" to 36'-1\"), and verification that all buildings fall under the 40' allowable height limit.\n\n*   **Block 17: table — Site Area Calculation (MF-2)**\n    A calculation of required area based on unit types. READ the block content to verify that the 94,192 sf provided site area satisfies the 93,600 sf required for the 39 2+ bedroom units.\n\n*   **Block 8: notes — Parking Data**\n    A tabular summary of parking requirements. READ the block content for the breakdown of vehicle parking (surface vs. garage) and the exact counts for short-term and long-term bicycle parking spaces.\n\n*   **Block 10: notes — Site Plan Release Notes**\n    A list of administrative conditions for the site plan. READ the block content for compliance requirements regarding standard building codes, signage, utility providers, demolition, and right-of-way excavation permits.\n\n*   **Block 4: notes — Accessibility Notes**\n    Design constraints for ADA compliance. READ the block content for specific, mandated slope tolerances for accessible parking spaces, cross-slopes, and ramps (e.g., max ramp slope of 1:12).\n\n*   **Block 2: drawing — Lighting Note & Figure 34**\n    Requirements for exterior lighting. READ for the requirement that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded per Subchapter E 2.5. Use VISION to review the four acceptable fixture profiles illustrated in Figure 34.\n\n*   **Block 6: notes — Fence Note**\n    Tree protection requirements. READ the block content for instructions mandating hand-digging of post holes within Critical Root Zones (CRZ) and the required application of 3-inch hardwood mulch.\n\n*   **Block 9: notes — Trash Collection Notes**\n    Rules for waste management. READ the block content for curbside unattended container restrictions and necessary enforcement signage requirements.\n\n*   **Blocks 3, 5, 7, 11, 12, 13: various — Administrative & Boilerplate Data**\n    Standard site plan identifiers and administrative data. READ for the legal description (Block 3), the specific benchmark elevations (Block 5), and general utility liability notes (Block 7). Use VISION to locate the graphic scale showing 1\" = 20' (Block 11), the Texas 811 call-before-you-dig logo (Block 12), and the engineer's seal dated 2/27/26 (Block 13).\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":63,"startLine":1,"totalLines":63}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776272591536,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01K3ybCBDBedWBD5qzbpBxWD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N1HFbZP9xc1gnkPBSq9Kbk","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1842,"cache_read_input_tokens":40828,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1842},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"210f7c66-b6ac-4c5e-b337-405e381dc733","uuid":"4d87188d-10fb-4c35-8b75-49106cb5de3e"}
{"level":30,"time":1776272591547,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N1HFbZP9xc1gnkPBSq9Kbk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n     4→\n     5→Lighting Note\n     6→All exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n     7→[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\n     8→Figure 34\n     9→Examples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n    10→\n    11→## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n    12→\n    13→Site Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n    14→\n    15→## Block 3: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n    16→\n    17→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    18→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    19→\n    20→## Block 4: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n    21→\n    22→Trash Collection Notes\n    23→Trash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\n    24→A restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\n    25→Reference: TCM, 9.4.2\n    26→\n    27→## Block 5: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n    28→\n    29→Accessibility Notes\n    30→1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n    31→2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n    32→3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n    33→4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n    34→5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n    35→\n    36→## Block 6: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n    37→\n    38→Parking Data\n    39→Land Use Residential\n    40→Parking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\n    41→Compact Spaces Provided 0\n    42→ADA Spaces Required X\n    43→ADA Spaces Provided X\n    44→Total Bicycle Spaces Required 5\n    45→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\n    46→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\n    47→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\n    48→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n    49→\n    50→## Block 7: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n    51→\n    52→a. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\n    53→b. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\n    54→c. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\n    55→d. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    56→e. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    57→f. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    58→g. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\n    59→h. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\n    60→i. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    61→\n    62→## Block 8: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    63→\n    64→Legal Description\n    65→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    66→\n    67→## Block 9: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n    68→\n    69→Fence Note\n    70→Post holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n    71→\n    72→## Block 10: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    73→\n    74→Benchmarks\n    75→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    76→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    77→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    78→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    79→\n    80→## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n    81→\n    82→0 20' 40'\n    83→Scale 1\" = 20'\n    84→\n    85→## Block 14: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n    86→\n    87→| Site Data Table | | |\n    88→| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n    89→| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n    90→| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n    91→| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n    92→| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n    93→| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n    94→| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n    95→| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n    96→| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n    97→| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n    98→| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n    99→| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n   100→| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n   101→| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n   102→\n   103→## Block 15: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n   104→\n   105→| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n   106→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   107→| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n   108→| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n   109→| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n   110→| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n   111→| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n   112→| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n   113→| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n   114→| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n   115→\n   116→## Block 16: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n   117→\n   118→| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n   119→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   120→| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n   121→| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n   122→| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n   123→| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n   124→| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n   125→\n   126→## Block 17: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n   127→\n   128→| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n   129→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   130→| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n   131→| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n   132→| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n   133→| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   134→| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n   135→| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n   136→| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   137→| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n   138→\n   139→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"210f7c66-b6ac-4c5e-b337-405e381dc733","uuid":"8e5a4ee4-25fb-4ca3-acf9-e5282a0731c8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n\nLighting Note\nAll exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\nFigure 34\nExamples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n\nSite Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n\n## Block 3: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 4: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n\nTrash Collection Notes\nTrash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\nA restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\nReference: TCM, 9.4.2\n\n## Block 5: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n\nAccessibility Notes\n1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n\n## Block 6: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n\nParking Data\nLand Use Residential\nParking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\nCompact Spaces Provided 0\nADA Spaces Required X\nADA Spaces Provided X\nTotal Bicycle Spaces Required 5\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n\n## Block 7: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n\na. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\nb. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\nc. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\nd. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\ne. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\nf. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\ng. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\nh. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\ni. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n\n## Block 8: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 9: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n\nFence Note\nPost holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n\n## Block 10: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n\n0 20' 40'\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 14: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n\n| Site Data Table | | |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n\n## Block 15: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n\n## Block 16: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n\n| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n\n## Block 17: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n\n","numLines":139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776272592015,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01HBmiGpd3BRTebR1hM3dgTC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FQyiKCSwwWJ7wTdmyPESFt","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision","input":{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":9,"prompt":"Please examine Sheet 9 (Site Plan) and describe:\n1. Are the existing utility poles and overhead utility lines shown from the existing conditions survey visible on this proposed site plan?\n2. Where are they located relative to the proposed buildings (Buildings 1-8)?\n3. Are there any dimension annotations or clearance notes showing horizontal or vertical clearances from the overhead lines to buildings, driveways, or other site features?\n4. Do the buildings, parking areas, driveways, or retaining walls appear to be positioned with adequate clearance from the overhead lines?\n5. Are there any notations about utility coordination, pole relocation, or clearance verification?\n\nThe site plan shows Buildings 1-8, driveways, parking, the detention pond, and retaining walls. Please assess how the proposed layout relates to the existing overhead facilities."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1245,"cache_read_input_tokens":46048,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1245},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa426fab-05eb-4a3a-b7ff-045b4f6362f9","uuid":"2209580a-61dd-4461-85ad-66969e2bd4e0"}
