{"level":30,"time":1776272740260,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjGdfBdXL1vcdGSUd1U8B5","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 38 — Wall Plan\n     2→\n     3→Plan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet provides the structural details, plan view, and specifications for retaining walls, including a 14'-0\" maximum height retaining wall section. Its primary purpose is to guide the construction, reinforcement, and inspection of the site's retaining walls, footings, and associated slab tie-ins.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values**:\n    10→* Maximum retaining wall height: 14'-0\"\n    11→* Concrete compressive strength: 3,000 PSI (minimum 28-day)\n    12→* Allowable soil bearing: 4,000 PSF\n    13→* Active soil pressure (heel): 45.5 PSF\n    14→* Passive pressure: 290 PSF\n    15→* Soil weight: 130 PSF\n    16→* Reinforcing steel grades: Grade 60 (for #4 rebar and larger), Grade 40 (for #3 rebar)\n    17→* Wall vertical reinforcement: #7 @ 10\" O.C.\n    18→* Wall horizontal reinforcement: #3 @ 12\" O.C. (each face)\n    19→* Minimum concrete clear cover: 3\" for surfaces in contact with earth; 1 1/2\" for walls/footings adjacent to exterior/interior surfaces; 1 1/2\" above sub-grade for slabs\n    20→* Dowel embedment: 6\" embedment for #6 rebar dowels into existing concrete\n    21→\n    22→**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n    23→\n    24→* **Block 2**: diagram — 14'-0\" Max Retaining Wall cross-section showing rebar spacing, gravel backfill, weep pipes, and geotextile placement. READ for exact rebar sizing and material specifications, VISION for structural layout and dimensions.\n    25→* **Block 4**: drawing — Plan View showing the layout of the new and existing retaining walls, pond walls, and specific doweling instructions for an existing buttress. Use VISION to understand the spatial relationship, alignment, and construction phasing between new and existing walls.\n    26→* **Block 1**: diagram — Dowel Detail showing a cross-section for tying a new slab to an existing slab with #6 rebar. READ for drill bit sizing (7/8\" for #4), epoxy installation instructions, and hand-installation notes.\n    27→* **Block 3**: diagram — Typical Step in Footing elevation view showing standard 2'-0\" horizontal and 12\" vertical step dimensions. Use VISION for step geometry and reinforcement continuity.\n    28→* **Block 10**: table — Design Criteria outlining IBC 2015 soil bearing and lateral pressure requirements. READ for the exact engineering design values.\n    29→* **Block 6**: notes — General and structural notes detailing concrete specifications, clear cover minimums, and reinforcement rules. READ for material grades, cover depths, and corner bar requirements (24\"x24\").\n    30→* **Block 9**: table — Required Inspections list. READ to confirm the three key inspection milestones (footings, walls, backfill/drainage).\n    31→* **Blocks 5, 7, 8**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains a general schematic disclaimer (Block 5), title block data for project 3759 Valley View (Block 7), and engineer seals for MLAW Engineers (Block 8).\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    36→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6450ffe4-612c-4eea-8b35-57aec9f3f591","uuid":"39eeb3e6-4283-4e1f-94f7-0ee628d635b8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-38/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 38 — Wall Plan\n\nPlan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides the structural details, plan view, and specifications for retaining walls, including a 14'-0\" maximum height retaining wall section. Its primary purpose is to guide the construction, reinforcement, and inspection of the site's retaining walls, footings, and associated slab tie-ins.\n\n**Key values**:\n* Maximum retaining wall height: 14'-0\"\n* Concrete compressive strength: 3,000 PSI (minimum 28-day)\n* Allowable soil bearing: 4,000 PSF\n* Active soil pressure (heel): 45.5 PSF\n* Passive pressure: 290 PSF\n* Soil weight: 130 PSF\n* Reinforcing steel grades: Grade 60 (for #4 rebar and larger), Grade 40 (for #3 rebar)\n* Wall vertical reinforcement: #7 @ 10\" O.C.\n* Wall horizontal reinforcement: #3 @ 12\" O.C. (each face)\n* Minimum concrete clear cover: 3\" for surfaces in contact with earth; 1 1/2\" for walls/footings adjacent to exterior/interior surfaces; 1 1/2\" above sub-grade for slabs\n* Dowel embedment: 6\" embedment for #6 rebar dowels into existing concrete\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n\n* **Block 2**: diagram — 14'-0\" Max Retaining Wall cross-section showing rebar spacing, gravel backfill, weep pipes, and geotextile placement. READ for exact rebar sizing and material specifications, VISION for structural layout and dimensions.\n* **Block 4**: drawing — Plan View showing the layout of the new and existing retaining walls, pond walls, and specific doweling instructions for an existing buttress. Use VISION to understand the spatial relationship, alignment, and construction phasing between new and existing walls.\n* **Block 1**: diagram — Dowel Detail showing a cross-section for tying a new slab to an existing slab with #6 rebar. READ for drill bit sizing (7/8\" for #4), epoxy installation instructions, and hand-installation notes.\n* **Block 3**: diagram — Typical Step in Footing elevation view showing standard 2'-0\" horizontal and 12\" vertical step dimensions. Use VISION for step geometry and reinforcement continuity.\n* **Block 10**: table — Design Criteria outlining IBC 2015 soil bearing and lateral pressure requirements. READ for the exact engineering design values.\n* **Block 6**: notes — General and structural notes detailing concrete specifications, clear cover minimums, and reinforcement rules. READ for material grades, cover depths, and corner bar requirements (24\"x24\").\n* **Block 9**: table — Required Inspections list. READ to confirm the three key inspection milestones (footings, walls, backfill/drainage).\n* **Blocks 5, 7, 8**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains a general schematic disclaimer (Block 5), title block data for project 3759 Valley View (Block 7), and engineer seals for MLAW Engineers (Block 8).\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776272741412,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01Sw2ni6D8KZ4s5sYq22do7Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Tree List blocks.md doesn't seem to have the actual tree species table in these blocks. The guide.md mentioned that Block 3 is the tree survey table. Let me use the vision tool to read that table from Sheet 32 to get the tree species list.\n\nAlso, I haven't found any detail sheets for transformers yet. Let me check if there are construction details about transformer pads, bollards, etc. The site plan guide mentioned sheets 22-28 have construction details. Let me look for any sheets that might have transformer-related details or equipment specifications.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":991,"cache_read_input_tokens":57081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":991},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"210f7c66-b6ac-4c5e-b337-405e381dc733","uuid":"7ebb8f1b-2efc-496d-8b8b-be99f3a9cc27"}
{"level":30,"time":1776272743715,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":3,"prompt":"Please examine Sheet 3 (General Notes) and specifically identify:\n1. Are there any notes or requirements related to electric transmission lines or distribution lines?\n2. Are there notes about overhead utility clearances, conductor clearances, or working near energized lines?\n3. Are there notes about notifications to Austin Energy or utility contractors?\n4. Are there any requirements related to 48-hour notice before work begins?\n5. What are the exact clearance requirements mentioned (if any) for work near electric transmission or distribution facilities?\n6. Are there blocks or sections labeled \"Electric Notes\", \"Electric Transmission Clearances\", or similar?\n\nThe guide.md indicates Block 14, 16, 18, and 20 contain electric-related notes, so please identify their exact content and requirements.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a detailed visual analysis of Sheet 3 (\"General Notes\" and \"Site Plan Release Notes\"), here are the answers to your specific questions:\n\n### 1. Are there any notes or requirements related to electric transmission lines or distribution lines?\nYes. The document contains two major, distinct sections dedicated entirely to this:\n*   **\"Electric Notes\"** (located in the left-center column) which primarily discusses easements, tree pruning, clearances, and overhead/underground electric facilities.\n*   **\"Electric Transmission Notes\"** (located in the top-center column) which contains a rigorous 16-point list of rules specifically governing construction, grading, and clearances around electric transmission structures.\n\n### 2. Are there notes about overhead utility clearances, conductor clearances, or working near energized lines?\nYes, strict overhead utility and energized line clearances are noted in multiple places:\n*   **Electric Transmission Notes (Item 3):** Specifically mandates, *\"OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"*\n*   **Electric Notes (Item 4):** States, *\"The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment.\"*\n\n### 3. Are there notes about notifications to Austin Energy or utility contractors?\nYes, explicit contacts and notification requirements are provided:\n*   **Electric Transmission Notes (Item 2):** *\"Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a meeting.\"*\n*   **Electric Transmission Notes (Item 3):** *\"Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (at 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction.\"*\n*   **Electric Transmission Notes (Item 9):** *\"Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff.\"*\n\n### 4. Are there any requirements related to 48-hour notice before work begins?\nYes. **Electric Transmission Notes (Item 3)** explicitly states:\n*\"A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction.\"*\n\n### 5. What are the exact clearance requirements mentioned for work near electric transmission or distribution facilities?\nThe following specific clearance distances are mandated throughout the notes:\n*   **10 feet:** Distance required from electric transmission structures for erecting barricades during construction *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 4)*.\n*   **10 feet:** Distance from the center line of proposed overhead electric facilities where the owner is responsible for initial tree pruning and removal *(Electric Notes, Item 3)*.\n*   **20 feet:** Minimum OSHA clearance required from energized transmission lines during construction *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 3)*.\n*   **20 feet:** Distance from transmission wire and/or structures where cranes, hoists, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging, or spoils areas are NOT permitted *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 7)*.\n*   **20 feet:** Minimum distance fire hydrants must be located from any transmission structure *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 13)*.\n*   **25 feet:** Minimum distance from the base of a transmission structure where trees cannot be planted *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 1)*.\n*   **25 feet:** Minimum distance from transmission structures (including down guy anchors) where digging or grading is prohibited *(Electric Transmission Notes, Items 2 & 15)*.\n*   **25 feet:** Minimum distance from transmission poles and structures where sprinklers are prohibited *(Electric Transmission Notes, Item 14)*.\n\n### 6. Exact Contents of the \"Electric Notes\" and \"Electric Transmission Notes\" Blocks\nHere is the verbatim character-by-character transcription of the requested sections:\n\n**Electric Notes**\n> 1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n> 2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n> 3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n> 4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n> 5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n\n**Electric Transmission Notes**\n> 1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n> 2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a meeting.\n> 3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (at 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n> 4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n> 5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n> 6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n> 7. No cranes, hoists, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n> 8. 24 hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n> 9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n> 10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n> 11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be mountable curbing.\n> 12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n> 13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n> 14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures.\n> 15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors.\n> 16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n>     - any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices.\n>     - any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, no\n>     - any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n\n*(Note: Immediately following item 16 in the \"Electric Transmission Notes\" column, there is an unnumbered block of text that exactly repeats the 5 points listed under the \"Electric Notes\" section verbatim.)*","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1479,"outputTokens":2060,"totalTokens":9078,"reasoningTokens":5539},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 3237656]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Please examine Sheet 3 (General Notes) and specifically identify:\n1. Are there any notes or requirements related to electric transmission lines or distribution lines?\n2. Are there notes about overhead utility clearances, conductor clearances, or working near energized lines?\n3. Are there notes about notifications to Austin Energy or utility contractors?\n4. Are there any requirements related to 48-hour notice before work begins?\n5. What are the exact clearance requirements mentioned (if any) for work near electric transmission or distribution facilities?\n6. Are there blocks or sections labeled \"Electric Notes\", \"Electric Transmission Clearances\", or similar?\n\nThe guide.md indicates Block 14, 16, 18, and 20 contain electric-related notes, so please identify their exact content and requirements."}]}],"headers":{"user-agent":"ai/5.0.152"}}},"response":{"id":"aitxt-xxsoErllKofwNDO69ZHKLTzB","timestamp":{},"modelId":"google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview","headers":{"access-control-allow-headers":"Content-Type, Authorization, ai-gateway-auth-method, ai-gateway-protocol-version, ai-model-id, ai-language-model-id, ai-language-model-specification-version, ai-image-model-specification-version, ai-embedding-model-specification-version, ai-language-model-streaming, http-referer, x-title, anthropic-beta