{"level":30,"time":1776273078135,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Please examine Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) and identify:\n\n1. Are there any trees (deciduous or evergreen plantings) shown on the plan near the overhead electric lines that run along the property boundaries?\n\n2. What tree species are proposed near the overhead distribution conductors (within 10 feet horizontally)?\n\n3. Are any trees shown within the utility easement areas?\n\n4. Can you identify the species names from tree callouts or legends? Do they appear to be utility-compatible species (typically marked \"UC\" in ECM Appendix F, generally mature height ≤20 feet)?\n\n5. Are there any trees shown beneath or in immediate proximity to the overhead lines marked \"- OHE -\"?\n\nThis is important for evaluating compliance with ECM § 2.4.1.D and § 2.4.2.C regarding tree placement near overhead conductors.","msg":"Calling vision tool: 1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777 (31)"}
{"level":30,"time":1776273082104,"pid":22798,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01AXpMtCjLCLUTDZvYzusS81","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I have very important information:\n\nFrom the Electric Review (document lists Austin Energy requirements):\n- **UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees** - This is referenced as a requirement\n\nThis confirms that Austin Energy has specific requirements for landscaping and trees in utility easements, and it cites UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4, which is the same code section (with slightly different section numbers) referenced in the grouping file.\n\nThe Electric Review references:\n- **Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual** - This is the ECM Appendix F that defines utility-compatible trees\n\nImportantly, there's a Bluebeam link to Austin Energy's site comments: https://studio.bluebeam.com/share/epcafnizivuefw\n\nHowever, I don't have direct access to download and review that Bluebeam file, so I cannot verify if there are any Austin Energy approvals or comments about tree clearances.\n\nFrom the City Arborist Review, I can see comments about tree preservation, CRZ (Critical Root Zone), and mitigation trees, but I don't see specific comments about electric utility clearances - the arborist comments focus on tree protection during construction and critical root zones.\n\nNow, let me summarize what I know and what I need to evaluate:\n\n**Key Findings So Far:**\n1. The overhead electric (OHE) line is shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) at the southern boundary\n2. Trees ARE shown planted at/near the southern boundary where the OHE line is\n3. The vision analysis shows trees planted \"directly adjacent to\" or \"directly south of the OHE line\"\n4. NO clearance zones or distance dimensions are shown on the landscape plan\n5. The plant schedule lists species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud, ornamental trees) but doesn't indicate which species are at which location on the plan\n\n\n6. Austin Energy compliance with UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 is required but no approval documentation appears on the landscape plan. The species planted near the OHE line cannot be verified from the plan, and no clearance dimensions are provided, making it impossible to confirm utility-compatibility or proper spacing without additional documentation.\n\n**Specific Evaluation for Each Checklist Item:**\n\nNow let me evaluate each checklist item:\n\n**EL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment**\n- Applicability: YES - OHE distribution line is present\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - The species of trees near the OHE line cannot be determined from the landscape plan. No clearance dimensions shown. Cannot verify if trees are utility-compatible without knowing their species.\n\n**EL-2.2: Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor or equipment**\n- Applicability: NO - Only distribution lines are present on this property per the facts provided; the 5' electric easements are distribution infrastructure, not transmission.\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.3: Large trees (mature height 40+ feet) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment**\n- Applicability: YES - OHE distribution line is present\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - Without identifying the specific species planted near the OHE line, I cannot determine if they qualify as large trees (40+ feet mature height per ECM Appendix F). Cedar Elm and Live Oak typically reach 40-50+ feet, which would trigger this criterion if located near the OHE line.\n\n**EL-2.4: Large trees (mature height 40+ feet) proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor or equipment**\n- Applicability: NO - No transmission lines on property\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.5: Trees (any species) proposed within transmission easement boundaries**\n- Applicability: NO - No transmission easement exists per facts.md\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.6: Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility pole**\n- Applicability: YES - OHE line presence implies poles are required\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - Specific pole locations are not labeled on the landscape plan, and no distance measurements are shown. The vision analysis did not identify pole positions, so distances cannot be verified.\n\n**EL-2.7: Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted electric equipment**\n- Applicability: Potentially - The Preliminary Electrical Design Plan (Sheet 21) references transformer pads, pull boxes, and meters, but their exact locations relative to proposed trees need verification.\n- Status: Uncertain - Cannot confirm whether pad-mounted equipment locations are shown on the landscape plan or if any trees fall within the 10-foot buffer.\n\n**EL-2.8: Trees proposed within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment**\n- Applicability: YES - Underground electric service is indicated in the electrical plan\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - Comparing underground equipment locations from the electrical plan to tree placements on the landscape plan requires detailed cross-reference of both documents.\n\n**EL-2.9: Trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers**\n- Applicability: YES if trees are within 20 feet\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - The landscape plan contains no root barrier specifications for any trees, making it impossible to confirm whether required barriers are present.\n\n**EL-2.10: Plant legend or plant schedule does not identify species of trees proposed near electric facilities**\n- Applicability: YES\n- Status: FAIL - Tree symbols appear on the landscape plan without a corresponding legend identifying species at specific locations. While Sheet 35 provides a plant schedule listing species and quantities, there's no way to determine which species are planted where. Trees near the overhead electric line on the southern boundary lack species identification.\n\n**EL-2.11: Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) not differentiated on plans to allow verification of clearances from overhead electric facilities**\n- Applicability: YES if large trees are proposed\n- Status: FAIL - The landscape plan fails to distinguish which trees will reach 40+ feet at maturity. Sheet 31's guide references tree mitigation size (trees > 1.5 caliper inches), but doesn't flag large mature-height trees separately from smaller species. This makes it impossible to verify that large trees stay outside prohibited zones.\n\n**EL-2.12: Tree clearances measured from utility pole centerline or structure instead of from outer electric conductor**\n- Applicability: Only if clearances are dimensioned on plans\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - No clearance dimensions appear between trees and the overhead electric line on the landscape plan, so the measurement methodology cannot be evaluated.\n\n**EL-2.13: Tree clearances measured from tree canopy instead of from tree trunk (planting location)**\n- Applicability: Only if clearances are dimensioned on plans\n- Status: Same issue as EL-2.12 - without dimensions shown, this cannot be assessed.\n\n**EL-2.14: Trees not shown on landscape plan in relation to overhead electric facilities, preventing clearance verification**\n- Applicability: YES\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - While trees and the OHE line both appear on the plan, critical information is missing: no distance dimensions, no clearance zones marked, and no species identification for trees near the line. The deficiency could interpret as either trees being absent from the plan entirely, or trees being shown but lacking sufficient detail to verify clearances. Given that trees are depicted but clearance verification is impossible due to missing species data and dimensions, the status is NOT-VERIFIABLE rather than outright FAIL. The trees appear positioned very close to the OHE line—potentially within the 15-foot prohibited zone for distribution conductors. However, the critical issue is the absence of any Austin Energy written approval documentation on the submitted plans. While I can't definitively confirm a violation without verified distances and species data, the missing approval letter is a significant concern. The Electric Review cites Austin Energy coordination requirements, yet no approval memo or letter appears in the plan set. is visible despite trees being planted adjacent to the utility facility. The guidelines are clear that any trees proposed near utility lines require explicit written Austin Energy approval, and I don't see documentation of this on the plan. However, without confirmed species and measured distances, I can't definitively establish whether these trees actually fall within prohibited zones. The most accurate assessment is NOT-VERIFIABLE since I lack the specific data needed to make a definitive determination.\n\nFor the items lacking species identification and distance dimensions—EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.6, and EL-2.15—the status should be NOT-VERIFIABLE. EL-2.10 fails because species aren't identified on the landscape plan itself. EL-2.11 presents a similar issue with large trees not being differentiated. EL-2.14 also cannot be verified due to insufficient clearance information. Now I need to check the electrical plan on Sheet 21 to locate the pad-mounted equipment and measure actual distances to the proposed trees.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4556,"cache_read_input_tokens":63127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4556},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d155abec-404b-4144-83fe-2ada3fa35846","uuid":"8b0891ce-3694-46ba-a14e-cf28d1d792de"}
